#{[17592186055369 "The line has slope $2$ and vertical intercept $-4$. It passes through $(0,-4)$ and $(2,0)$, so choose the graph with these intercepts and positive slope $2$."] [17592186055429 "Substituting $x=0,1,2,3$ into $y=-x+1$ gives $y=1,0,-1,-2$. The required graph passes through these four points and slopes downward."] [17592186055430 "The equation $y=\\frac12x-2$ has vertical intercept $-2$ and horizontal intercept $4$. Choose the line through $(0,-2)$ and $(4,0)$."] [17592186059526 "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\vec{a} + 6\\vec{b} &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060025 "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle12,5\\rangle-\\langle9,-2\\rangle=\\langle12-9,5-(-2)\\rangle=\\langle3,7\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060028 "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle4,1\\rangle-\\langle1,-4\\rangle=\\langle3,5\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060041 "Multiply each component by $3$:\n$$3\\langle8,-7\\rangle=\\langle24,-21\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060046 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10-28)\\mathbf{i}-(-3-16)\\mathbf{j}+(-21-40)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-18\\mathbf{i}+19\\mathbf{j}-61\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186060047 "The source asks for the cross product. Expanding its determinant gives\n$$\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v=\\left\\langle9(3)-6(-3),\\ 6(0)-\\frac13(3),\\ \\frac13(-3)-9(0)\\right\\rangle=\\langle45,-1,-1\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060071 "Expand the two squares. Their mixed terms cancel, so $$(\\sin x-\\cos x)^2+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=2\\sin^2 x+2\\cos^2 x=2.$$"] [17592186060072 "On the domain of the original expression, rationalize and simplify:\n$$\\frac{\\cos x}{1-\\sin x}-\\tan x=\\frac{1+\\sin x}{\\cos x}-\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}=\\frac1{\\cos x}=\\sec x.$$"] [17592186060118 "First scale, then add components:\n$$\\langle-2,1\\rangle+2\\langle4,-1\\rangle=\\langle-2+8,1-2\\rangle=\\langle6,-1\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060124 "Compute $2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c=\\langle6,6\\rangle-\\langle-24,12\\rangle=\\langle30,-6\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c)=\\langle4,-2\\rangle-\\langle30,-6\\rangle=\\langle-26,4\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060125 "Compute $2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c=\\langle8,2\\rangle+\\langle-5,10\\rangle=\\langle3,12\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c)=\\langle1,1\\rangle-\\langle3,12\\rangle=\\langle-2,-11\\rangle.$$"] [17592186060193 "The source condition is the magnitude of a cross product. Compute\n$$\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\langle-7x,11x,-5\\rangle.$$\nSquaring the magnitude equation gives $170x^2+25=705$, so $x^2=4$ and $x=\\pm2$."] [17592186060194 "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6x-8)\\mathbf{j}+(0-2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-(6x-8),-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-(6x-8))^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36{x}^{2}-96x+64+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77} & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+77 & =13 \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+64 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186060226 "Use a common denominator and the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\cos\\theta+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}=\\sec\\theta.$$"] [17592186060343 "Where the original denominator is nonzero,\n$$\\cos^2\\theta\\left(1+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\theta}\\right)=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.$$"] [17592186060344 "Factor out $\\cos\\theta$:\n$$\\cos^3\\theta+\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)=\\cos\\theta.$$"] [17592186060345 "On the domain of the original fraction,\n$$\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta+\\sin^4\\theta}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}{\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}=\\tan\\theta.$$"] [17592186060346 "For the original expression, $\\sin x\\ne0$. Factoring its denominator gives\n$$\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin^3x+\\sin x\\cos^2x}=\\frac{\\sin^2x}{\\sin x(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)}=\\sin x.$$"] [17592186060349 "Factor and apply the Pythagorean identity:\n$$\\sin^2x\\cos^3x+\\sin^4x\\cos x=\\sin^2x\\cos x(\\cos^2x+\\sin^2x)=\\sin^2x\\cos x.$$"] [17592186060392 "Collect the corresponding components:\n$$(7\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j)+(3\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)=10\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j.$$"] [17592186060393 "Distribute the scalar and collect components:\n$$2(4\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j)+(6\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j)=14\\mathbf i.$$"] [17592186064712 "An antiderivative of $x^3$ is $x^4/4$. Thus\n$$\\int_{-2}^{2}x^3\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_{-2}^{2}=4-4=0.$$"] [17592186064714 "Since $x\\ge0$ on the interval, $\\sqrt{x^5}=x^{5/2}$. Hence\n$$\\int_0^4\\sqrt{x^5}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac27x^{7/2}\\right]_0^4=\\frac27(128)=\\frac{256}{7}.$$"] [17592186064853 "Apply the power rule:\n$$\\int_1^2x^{-4}\\,dx=\\left[-\\frac1{3x^3}\\right]_1^2=-\\frac1{24}+\\frac13=\\frac7{24}.$$"] [17592186064855 "On this nonnegative interval, the integrand is $x^{3/5}$. Thus\n$$\\int_0^1\\sqrt[5]{x^3}\\,dx=\\left[\\frac58x^{8/5}\\right]_0^1=\\frac58.$$"] [17592186083545 "At $t=-1$, the point is $(0,1)$, while $x'(t)=4t^3+1=-3$ and $y'(t)=2t=-2$. The tangent slope is $2/3$, so the normal slope is $-3/2$. The normal is $y=1-3x/2$; setting $y=0$ gives $x=2/3$."] [17592186083547 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t},\\hspace{2em}{y}^{'}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{'}(t)}{{x}^{'}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186083614 "Since $dx/dt=1$ and $dy/dt=3t^2$, we have $dy/dx=3t^2$. A horizontal tangent requires $dy/dx=0$, which gives $t=0$."] [17592186083913 "At $t=-1$ the point is $(3,-1)$. The tangent slope is\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5t^4}{2t-2}=-\\frac54.$$\nHence its equation is $y+1=-\\frac54(x-3)$."] [17592186088020 "Eliminating the parameter gives\n$$(x+3)^2+(y-5)^2=9^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)=81.$$\nThis is a circle centered at $(-3,5)$ with radius $9$."] [17592186088021 "The standard counterclockwise circle of radius $8$ about the origin is $x=8\\cos t$, $y=8\\sin t$. At $t=0$ it starts at $(8,0)$, and its initial velocity points upward as required."] [17592186088022 "The parametrization $x=6\\cos t$, $y=6\\sin t$ satisfies $x^2+y^2=36$, giving the circle of radius $6$ about the origin."] [17592186088030 "The square root requires $t\\ge0$. Therefore $y=\\sqrt t\\ge0$ and $x=t(t+6)\\ge0$, so the curve lies in the first quadrant together with the origin."] [17592186088031 "Complete the square:\n$$x=t^2-4t=(t-2)^2-4\\ge-4.$$\nThe lower bound is attained at $t=2$."] [17592186088269 "Substitute the indicated parameter values: $a=(-2)^2+(-2)=2$, $b=0^2-0=0$, and $c=3^2+3=12$. Thus $a+b+c=14$."] [17592186088272 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =9({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$"] [17592186088281 "Using $-1\\le\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta\\le1$, we obtain $-4\\le x\\le2$ and $0\\le y\\le4$."] [17592186088355 "Translate the standard radius-$2$ circle to center $(-1,2)$:\n$$x=2\\cos t-1,\\qquad y=2\\sin t+2.$$\nAt $t=0$ it starts at $(1,2)$ and moves counterclockwise."] [17592186088368 "The displayed arc lies in the third quadrant, from $(-4,0)$ to $(0,-4)$. In $x=4\\cos\\theta$, $y=4\\sin\\theta$, these endpoints occur at $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=3\\pi/2$, respectively. Use $\\pi\\le\\theta\\le3\\pi/2$."] [17592186101208 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186101209 "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [17592186101210 "From $x=t-2$ we have $t=x+2$. Substitution into $y=t^2$ gives $y=(x+2)^2$, with no restriction on real $x$."] [17592186106126 "The source asks for $\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)$. Compute\n$$\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c=\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle,$$\nthen $\\langle3,1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle=-66+8-6=-64$."] [17592186106127 "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{q},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106128 "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (3\\mathbf{b}\\times (-2\\mathbf{c})) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =24 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106129 "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106130 "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106131 "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120272 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}2t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120274 "For $t>0$ the speed simplifies as\n$$\\sqrt{t^{-2}+4+4t^2}=2t+\\frac1t.$$\nThus the length is\n$$\\int_1^e\\left(2t+\\frac1t\\right)dt=[t^2+\\ln t]_1^e=(e^2+1)-1=e^2.$$"] [17592186120275 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}t+t\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t-t\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t+(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-(\\operatorname{cos}t-t\\operatorname{sin}t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.167em}t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120281 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120527 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\hspace{0.222em}6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120528 "First, we find ${\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\hspace{0.222em}3⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{t})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9t+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3(t+1)^{1/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124972 "At $(0,1)$ the function value is $0$. Setting $1-4x^2-y^2=0$ gives the level curve $4x^2+y^2=1$."] [17592186129512 "The image is the full disk of radius $\\sqrt5$. With $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, the integrand becomes $r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta$. Hence use\n$$\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{\\sqrt5}r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin^2\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186129513 "The disk boundary becomes $r^2-6r\\cos\\theta=0$. Thus $-\\pi/2\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$ and $0\\le r\\le6\\cos\\theta$. Also $(x^2-y^2)dA=r^3\\cos2\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta$. The integral is\n$$\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{6\\cos\\theta}r^3\\cos2\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186129516 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b194b4882dcada56a10ec69f9dae80b363ea05efaae27385ceacc9a15ba3b56f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $5$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=6+y=6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 6+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )z\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-\\pi /2}}}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6+r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129517 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/75/75bec31176c369e69d5b11af3eeab23f86a4f32251e37f56b225e9dd0bd4a3de.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2+{r}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+{r}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2+{r}^{2}}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129518 "In cylindrical coordinates the integral is\n$$\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^2\\int_0^{12-4r\\sin\\theta}r^2\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$\nAfter integrating in $z$, the sine term integrates to zero over a full turn. The result is $12(2\\pi)(8/3)=64\\pi$."] [17592186129519 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}((r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi /2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129626 "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }2r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }2r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}[{r}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186129628 "The circle becomes $r=2\\cos\\theta$; restricting to $y\\ge0$ gives $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Since $x\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\,dA=r^3\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, use\n$$\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^{2\\cos\\theta}r^3\\cos\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"] [17592186129631 "Separate the two factors:\n$$\\int_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}\\int_1^2r^2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\left[\\frac{r^3}3\\right]_1^2[-\\cos\\theta]_{\\pi/3}^{\\pi/2}=\\frac73\\cdot\\frac12=\\frac76.$$"] [17592186129739 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/9c/9c4ea0ba92e19a201e3944defa6deb0bc1ee76971adaa809521722b6ac31241f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $3$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=10-x=10-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le z\\le 10-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}}{\\int }_{1}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}10-r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}z}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134065 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y\\operatorname{cos}x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-\\operatorname{cos}x+3y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134066 "Differentiate the corresponding components:\n$$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=e^x(\\cos x-\\sin x)+x^2e^{xy}.$$\nAt $(0,1)$ this is $1$."] [17592186134094 "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle=\\langle xy,y\\rangle$, the scalar curl is $Q_x-P_y=0-x=-x$."] [17592186134099 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134135 "The divergence is\n$$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\partial(xz)}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial(y^2)}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial1}{\\partial z}=z+2y.$$\nThe Divergence Theorem therefore makes the missing integrand $z+2y$."] [17592186134136 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}y-2z}}}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{y}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134137 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}4\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\hspace{0.167em}000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134138 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134139 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & =2z+0+0 \\\\  & =2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134140 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134368 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186134369 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F})(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
